Rule <p>Put the primary key of the "1" side as a foreign key in the "N" side.</p> <p>This is the most common mapping — every <code>EMPLOYEE</code> has one <code>dno</code>.</p>   ???+ info "Example: `EMPLOYEE WORKS_FOR DEPARTMENT` (N:1)"     <pre><code>ALTER TABLE EMPLOYEE
    ADD COLUMN dno INT,
    ADD FOREIGN KEY (dno) REFERENCES DEPARTMENT(dnumber);
</code></pre>   !!! tip "Total participation ⇒ NOT NULL"     Because every employee must work for a department (total participation), add `NOT NULL`:      <pre><code>ALTER TABLE EMPLOYEE ADD COLUMN dno INT NOT NULL;
</code></pre>

Three kinds of subquery Kind Returns Used in Scalar One value Comparison (<code>=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>) Row One row Row comparison Table Many rows <code>IN</code>, <code>EXISTS</code>, <code>ANY</code>, <code>ALL</code>   ???+ info "Scalar subquery example"     <pre><code>SELECT name FROM EMPLOYEE
WHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM EMPLOYEE);
</code></pre>      The subquery returns one number (the company average); the outer query compares against it.     ???+ info "Correlated subquery"     <pre><code>SELECT name FROM EMPLOYEE e
WHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno = e.dno);
</code></pre>      The subquery references `e.dno` from the outer query — it is **re-executed per outer row**.     !!! warning "Correlated subqueries are expensive"     Naïve execution is $O(n \times m)$ — one subquery per outer row. Query optimizers often rewrite them as joins.

The four quantifiers Operator Meaning <code>IN</code> Value is in the subquery result <code>NOT IN</code> Value is not in the subquery result <code>EXISTS</code> Subquery returns at least one row <code>NOT EXISTS</code> Subquery returns no rows <code>&gt; ANY</code> Greater than at least one <code>&gt; ALL</code> Greater than all   ???+ info "Example"     <pre><code>-- Employees who earn more than any employee in dept 5
SELECT name FROM EMPLOYEE
WHERE salary &gt; ANY (SELECT salary FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno = 5);

-- Employees who earn more than every employee in dept 5
SELECT name FROM EMPLOYEE
WHERE salary &gt; ALL (SELECT salary FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno = 5);
</code></pre>   !!! warning "NULL and NOT IN"     `NOT IN (subquery)` returns **no rows** if the subquery returns any NULL. This is a notorious SQL trap.      <pre><code>-- Broken: returns empty because dno has a NULL
SELECT * FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno NOT IN (SELECT dno FROM DEPARTMENT);
</code></pre>

NULL is unknown <p>NULL is not a value — it means unknown or does not exist.</p>   ???+ info "Three-valued logic"     Every boolean expression evaluates to **TRUE**, **FALSE**, or **UNKNOWN**:      | Expression | Result |     |---|---|     | `NULL = NULL` | UNKNOWN |     | `NULL &gt; 5` | UNKNOWN |     | `NULL AND TRUE` | UNKNOWN |     | `NULL OR TRUE` | TRUE |     | `NOT NULL` | UNKNOWN |     ???+ info "Testing for NULL"     <pre><code>-- Correct
SELECT * FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno IS NULL;
SELECT * FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno IS NOT NULL;

-- WRONG — always UNKNOWN (never matches)
SELECT * FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno = NULL;
</code></pre>   !!! warning "The NOT IN trap again"     <pre><code>-- Returns empty if any dno in DEPARTMENT is NULL
SELECT * FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE dno NOT IN (SELECT dno FROM DEPARTMENT);
</code></pre>      **Fix:** use `NOT EXISTS` instead.

INSERT <pre><code>-- Insert one row
INSERT INTO EMPLOYEE (ssn, name, salary, dno)
VALUES ('999999999', 'Alice', 50000, 5);

-- Insert from a query
INSERT INTO HIGH_EARNERS (ssn, name)
SELECT ssn, name FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE salary &gt; 100000;
</code></pre>   ???+ note "UPDATE"     <pre><code>UPDATE EMPLOYEE
SET    salary = salary * 1.10
WHERE  dno = 5;
</code></pre>      **Without WHERE, every row is updated.**     ???+ note "DELETE"     <pre><code>DELETE FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE ssn = '999999999';
</code></pre>      **Without WHERE, every row is deleted.**     !!! warning "Constraint violations"     - **INSERT** with duplicate PK → `UNIQUE` violation.     - **INSERT** with missing FK → `FOREIGN KEY` violation.     - **DELETE** a department with employees → `FOREIGN KEY` violation (unless CASCADE).     - **UPDATE** a PK that others reference → `FOREIGN KEY` violation (unless CASCADE).

What an index does <p>An index is a data structure (usually a B-tree) that speeds up lookups on a column.</p>   ???+ info "Creating an index"     <pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_employee_salary ON EMPLOYEE(salary);
CREATE INDEX idx_employee_dno    ON EMPLOYEE(dno);
</code></pre>   ???+ info "The EXPLAIN command"     <pre><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE
SELECT name FROM EMPLOYEE WHERE salary &gt; 50000;
</code></pre>      Shows the query plan (index scan vs sequential scan) and the actual timing.     !!! tip "When to add an index"     - Frequently filtered columns (`WHERE`).     - Frequently joined columns (foreign keys).     - Frequently sorted columns (`ORDER BY`).      But **every index slows down writes** — balance carefully.
